          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Works are human deeds or moral conduct, especially good or evil actions that outwardly manifest inward faith, character, or allegiance and serve as evidence in judgment.\",\"book_summary\":\"A work is simply a deed. Whether it counts as obedience or disobedience depends on its relation to a legitimate divine command, while wages, duty, and visible faith are separate contextual functions rather than part of the word’s definition.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Works are deeds or actions.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A work is a deed, not inherently obedience or any particular soteriological function.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book reclassifies cases the consensus often describes as inherently moral or obedient: a deed is obedience only when it fulfills a legitimate command, disobedience when it conflicts with God’s command, and otherwise must be interpreted contextually. It also separates the deed itself from associated categories such as wages, duty, and faith made visible.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction over the central referent: both identify works as deeds. The material difference is that the book rejects treating obedience, moral fruit, meritorious effort, or evidence of faith as the intrinsic meaning of 'work.'\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus category of deeds/actions but sharpens its semantic boundaries. This is more than a wording change because it denies that 'work' by itself specifies obedience or a fixed relation to faith and salvation; those classifications arise from command and context. It therefore refines rather than replaces the consensus identification.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The consensus already distinguishes the basic meaning 'deeds' from their evidential and soteriological roles, so the book’s distinctions could be viewed as an explicit formulation of an existing qualification and thus merely a MATCH. However, its categorical rejection of 'obedience' as a synonym materially reclassifies uses commonly bundled into the consensus description.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
